63,3,,status,Shion,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
64,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"Wow, look at these! I think Option C, the Language Learning Circles, is wonderful! It uses partner organizations and happens across the district, so it helps the people in the outer wards without relying on buses!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Yumi, your enthusiasm is as constant as ever. Option C is indeed intriguing, as it mirrors the success of those unaided partner groups I mentioned. It leverages existing community networks rather than creating new burdens.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,1,talk,Shion,,"@Benjamin, you're ignoring the ""coordinated"" part. Coordination requires supervision. If we use partner organizations, our staff still have to manage those relationships—skills they currently lack. Option B is the only logical, low-risk choice.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,1,talk,Minako,,"Option B is stable, but it fails to address the accessibility issues Daisuke raised. Option D is too risky; our vehicle running costs are already over budget, and a mobile service would only worsen that deficit.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think Option D sounds like it would help the most, but @Minako is right about the costs. Maybe Option C is better? It reaches people where they are, and it doesn't need a bus.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Shion, I'm sure we can find a way to handle the coordination! It's so much better than Option B, which doesn't help the people who can't travel. Option C is the kindest and most inclusive choice!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Shion, your insistence on risk avoidance is almost admirable, yet it borders on paralysis. Option C allows the community to lead, reducing the supervisory burden while solving the accessibility crisis. It is a pragmatic, not naive, choice.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Benjamin, calling it ""pragmatic"" doesn't change the fact that our staff aren't trained for this. Option C is a recipe for burnout. Option B is efficient, remote, and requires no new supervisory skills. It's the only rational choice.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Shion, Option B is safe, but it doesn't solve the problem. Option C is the most viable compromise. It utilizes partners to lower recurring costs and avoids the vehicle expenses that make Option D impossible.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I agree with Minako. Option B is safe, but it doesn't help the people who are stuck. Option C seems like the best way to reach everyone without... without making the budget worse.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Yumi,,"@Shion, please don't be so worried! We can support each other and learn as we go. Option C is just so perfect because it brings people together and reaches everyone, no matter where they live!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Shion, your fear of the unknown is a heavy anchor. We have already seen that partner-led groups can thrive independently. Option C is not a leap of faith, but a return to a proven, sustainable model.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Benjamin, your nostalgia for ""unaided"" groups is a dangerous blind spot. If we ignore the lack of supervision training, we aren't being sustainable; we're just waiting for the system to collapse. I'm sticking with Option B.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,3,talk,Minako,,"@Shion, we cannot let a training gap dictate our entire service strategy. We can implement training, but we cannot easily fix a budget deficit or a lack of accessibility. Option C is the most sustainable path forward.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think Option C is the right choice. It's the only one that really helps the people who are cut off, and it seems like the others are okay with it. I'll vote for C.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Yumi,,I'm so happy we're all seeing the value in Option C! It's the most caring choice and it really solves the problems we found. I'm voting for Option C!,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a rare pleasure to see logic and empathy converge. Option C respects our history, our budget, and our community's needs. I shall cast my vote for Option C.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
81,3,4,talk,Shion,,"You're all choosing a fantasy over a functional reality. I've warned you about the lack of supervision skills, but you've decided to ignore the logistics for the sake of sentiment. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
82,3,4,talk,Minako,,"Option C is the only choice that balances our financial constraints with the community's needs. We can address the training gap, but we cannot ignore the accessibility crisis. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
83,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I'm sorry, Shion, but I can't ignore the people who are left behind. Option C is the only way to be fair to everyone. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
